<blockquote>
<p>But all of them being related to other skips was disappointing for reasons I can't quite explain. It felt like a rug-pull somehow.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I won't necessarily call it a rug-pull. Besides, two scenarios do not directly involve SCPs (and instead, other ways one can be KIA). Relating the deaths to other articles, I feel, expresses a message. These characters died for a greater good, to save the many by offering themselves. And in return, they suffer pain (forever).</p>
<p>And in the words of Monty Python's <em>Life of Brian</em>, while it is nice to be saved by a Messiah, being nailed onto a piece of wood and left to die is not good for the person being nailed.</p>
<p>Besides, it provides something to be said across the setting and this is the scale that is most deserving of this article's circumstances.</p>
<p>EDIT: Mind you, I can indeed conjure &quot;original&quot; scenarios (like the first and last one). However, I do not feel that the impact will be as resonating if all scenarios are things that a reader has no idea of. And frankly, the only reason why the first and last scenario is original is because I could not find a suitable death set in the early 1900s.</p>

