			<div class="content" id="post-content-2775594">
									

<p>Going to have to agree with TL333s; I was hyped going through the main body of the article, but my curiosity evaporated with the addenda.</p>
<p>I can't say I like the twist that these are reincarnated Foundation operatives. It invites a bunch of difficult to answer questions about where exactly the line between straightforward death and traumatic reincarnation lies, such as 'what makes the Foundation special enough that only their members reincarnate', 'why do so many different, seemingly unconnected anomalies all cause reincarnation', 'why do such different traumas produce the same stereotyped set of reactions'. It sort of ends up implicitly suggesting a unified theory of anomalies, which is a suggestion I never really like. It also kind of reminded me of Bell Weather, which I think gets away with the same thing because it's not constrained by the emphasis on explanations which the SCP format sets up, and because it straightforwardly implies that all Foundation personnel who die in the line of duty are cursed. This seems to be suggesting that dying for the Foundation just happens to be so awful that it traumatises the soul permanently, and given the examples (death by Brokenite torturers, death by absorption, blown up by experimental weapons, getting wished out of existence) I don't buy that only Foundation people would come back, or would be traumatised so severely by those deaths that they're a special case.</p>
<p>The fact that this is the last part of the article hampered the delivery. It starts of so well, with a strong idea and concept which both inspires curiosity and is deeply messed up. The question of 'what could scare an unborn child into resisting birth' is a fascinating one and works as a really strong hook into the second part of the article, and going into the addenda I expected this to be an easy upvote. Unfortunately, it felt like I was being given a bunch of vignettes which were neither strong enough to stand alone nor coherent enough to improve when taken together. Having all the deaths be due to other SCPs seemed like a little bit of a cop-out. I had expected going in that there would be new material, but the content that's given is a handful regurgitated older articles that seem to have been picked at random, with no real dramatic development within or across them save for -5 and -48 being the same person. The short interview snippets just become clipped retellings of other stories that I couldn't get much out of.</p>

							</div>

								
					
